-| DT-C67 | VIEW-078 what a Stop in the Ports popover may end (ports-stop, replacing PR #42) | A Stop ends only the program the row showed: the process id and its start time must agree on the row, in a fresh scan and once more right before the kill, or it is refused as stale ("process N now belongs to another program"). Every holder of the port is checked before anything is ended: a port also held outside the workspace, by Kolux itself or by a system process is refused whole. The workspace folder must be at least two folders deep, no home folder (USERPROFILE, HOMEDRIVE+HOMEPATH, HOME, the terminals' home) and no parent of one, a Git worktree on disk and a workspace the project store lists (or, failing that, one Git lists for the project whose folder holds it; Git is asked only then, for that one project). The cheap checks run first. The chain from the program up to the pane's shell must be real link by link: Windows keeps a dead parent's id on its orphans and reuses ids, so a stranger can read as a pane's child; a link whose parent started later than its child, or whose start time the system withholds, refuses the stop ("process N was not started from a terminal of this workspace"). The kill holds the program open (Windows never reuses a pid while a handle is open; a program that cannot be opened is refused, "process N could not be opened", unless it is already gone), reads the third start time from that handle, reads the parent table only then (the scan's is stale by the time the guards are through), holds each child as its start time is read, ends only the children the same rule confirms (never a process a stale parent-id field hangs under the program, as `taskkill /T` would) and that a second table, read while they are all held, still lists under the same parent (a child whose pid changed hands between the two readings is newer than the program by construction, so only the second reading can tell; it is left out with everything under it), deepest first, then the 